From f4218b4b50ac8b843b35ac3723410d7407baa62f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 20:37:47 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Let's Encrypt: always listed on the DDNS page, and a
 certificate by DNS validation (102.8 line)

- Reaper_DDNS.asp lists Let's Encrypt at all times. It stays greyed, with the existing hint, until
  DDNS is on with a provider; it used to be hidden then, which read as removed.
- New: Let's Encrypt via DNS (rc/reaper_ledns.c). The router asks Let's Encrypt with the stock
  acme.sh and the DNS provider's own upstream script (Cloudflare, deSEC, Duck DNS), so it needs no
  DDNS, no open port 80 and no inbound traffic, and the name does not have to point at the router.
  The certificate goes in through the imported-certificate path (le_enable=2 only once the files
  exist), so the web server, Encrypted DNS and the backups treat it as an uploaded one. A daily
  cron line renews it; switching off removes the account, the token and the keys. The token
  reaches acme.sh in its environment only, never a command line or a log. With Warden filtering
  the router's own traffic, the provider API and Let's Encrypt are let through for the length of a
  run.
- acme.sh installs dns_cf, dns_desec and dns_duckdns. nvram reaper_ledns_*; backup section cert.
Also in these files: rc.h and rc/Makefile carry the conntrack-helper and DNS-rewrite declarations;
rc/services.c the rewrite include, the helper rules and avahi wide-area off; shared/defaults.c
nf_ftp=0.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
